From 876f9669da488a9f9a508dfa03740fe8f49b2586 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Youll <andrewyoull86@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:02:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] yuv420p video without conversions: playing, dissolving,
 exporting, colours

Profiled on Linux with MLT built without the xine module (as on RISC OS),
playing 1080p H.264 asked for as yuv420p, swscale took more time than
decoding:

- The avdeinterlace filter, which the loader attaches to every clip when
  the xine module's deinterlace isn't built, asked every frame for yuv422,
  so progressive yuv420p video went yuv420p -> yuv422 -> yuv420p. It now
  passes the format asked for through, and converts a frame to yuv422
  only when that frame is to be deinterlaced.
- The avformat producer made a swscale context and ran it for every
  frame even when nothing was to change (same format, colour space and
  range): it now copies the planes.
- The luma transition dissolved only in yuv422 (or RGBA): it now
  dissolves two opaque yuv420p pictures plane by plane, with the yuv422
  path's arithmetic, when yuv420p is asked for.

- The avformat consumer asked for yuv422 whatever the encoder took: it
  now asks for yuv420p when the encoder takes yuv420p, and copies the
  planes when nothing is to change.
- The colour producer made its yuv picture in BT.601 whatever the
  profile, which an HD (BT.709) export converted through RGB: it now makes
  it in the profile's colour space.

- Two options (off by default) cut whole-picture copies:
  MLT_AVFORMAT_CACHE_DEFAULT sets the image cache's size when nothing else
  does (0 on RISC OS: the cache copies each picture in, and again out),
  and MLT_AVFORMAT_CONCEAL_BY_REFERENCE keeps the last good decoded
  picture for error concealment by reference, converting it only when a
  picture fails, instead of a deep copy of every converted picture.

On the Linux test machine: a 1080p H.264 clip went from 19.6 to 6.6 ms a
picture, a dissolve between two such clips from about 25 to 9 ms, and
exporting 100 frames of a colour at 1080p from 6.1 to 1.0 s.
